{
  "answer": "Paragraph 10 and footnote 1 support continuing, ultimate Product Provider responsibility despite outsourcing. They do not supply a complete test for fulfillment. A justified conclusion would require an applicable legal standard and evidence addressing three distinct matters: retained authority and accountability, actual supervisory conduct, and operational soundness and proper ownership records over the relevant period. Contractual allocation, a logged supervision event, or a completed reconciliation alone does not establish all three. The supplied material establishes neither an entity’s compliance nor a definitive discharge standard.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "inference": "Text: outsourcing does not transfer away the Product Provider’s ultimate responsibility. Deduction: appointing a service provider cannot by itself establish fulfillment.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "Applicability of the retained edition to the product, activity and assessment period, including relevant conditions or modifications.",
        "Evidence that the assessed entity is the relevant Product Provider.",
        "Contracts, governance arrangements and actual practice showing who holds decision, information, intervention and remediation authority."
      ],
      "proposition": "Responsibility remains with the Product Provider despite outsourced performance.",
      "status": "Text-supported; factual application unresolved",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u38.3432"
        },
        {
          "quote": "the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Deduction: allocation of performance and retention of ultimate responsibility are different questions. Judgment: contractual reservations and governance powers are relevant evidence, but the quoted provisions do not declare them a sufficient compliance test.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "An applicable standard specifying what retained responsibility requires in substance.",
        "Evidence that reserved powers are usable and exercised when needed.",
        "The identity and capacity of the register keeper; Product Provider status does not alone establish that role."
      ],
      "proposition": "Authority allocation can support a finding of retained responsibility without proving adequate conduct or outcomes.",
      "status": "Source-grounded deduction; sufficiency unresolved",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "The scheme, or in the case of a unit trust, the trustee or the person so appointed by the trustee must maintain a register of holders.",
          "unit_id": "ut-current.parent"
        },
        {
          "quote": "the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text: footnote 1 directs attention to applicable, non-exhaustive factors. Judgment: supervision should be assessed through its substance, coverage and response to identified risks, rather than merely the existence of a recorded event. UT 6.11C supplies an express supervision standard for outsourced valuation, but transferring it to all tokenisation functions requires a separate warrant.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The applicable supervisory standard, including required scope, frequency, competence and response.",
        "Evidence of information reviewed, risks identified, escalation, intervention and completed remediation throughout the period.",
        "For reliance on UT 6.11C, applicability to the management company and outsourced valuation activity."
      ],
      "proposition": "Recorded supervisory conduct is relevant evidence, not an automatic finding of adequate supervision.",
      "status": "Conditional evaluative proposal",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "For factors of consideration, please also refer to Part A of the Appendix to the Tokenised Securities Circular, where applicable, when formulating the tokenisation arrangement.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        },
        {
          "quote": "robust and properly maintained policies and procedures, systems and controls underpinning the operation of the Tokenised Securities",
          "unit_id": "tokenised-securities.appendix"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The valuation activities of such third party should be subject to ongoing supervision and periodic review by the management company.",
          "unit_id": "ut-current.parent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text: paragraph 11 separately requires proper ownership-interest records and operational compatibility. Deduction: reconciliation completion establishes an activity, not necessarily accurate records reflecting legally valid interests. Judgment: results, discrepancies and their resolution must be assessed against an applicable standard; neither an isolated successful outcome nor an incident alone determines fulfillment of paragraph 10.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "A justified standard for operational soundness and proper ownership records, including the significance of defects and remediation.",
        "The legal relationship between tokens, underlying interests, authoritative records and settlement finality.",
        "Evidence of record completeness and accuracy, reconciliation coverage and results, outstanding exceptions, system compatibility and operational incidents across the relevant period."
      ],
      "proposition": "Operational and record-keeping outcomes require substantive assessment distinct from authority allocation and supervisory activity.",
      "status": "Text-supported requirements; fulfillment unresolved",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should ensure that proper records of token holders’ ownership interests in the product are maintained and the tokenisation arrangement is operationally compatible with service providers involved2.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u41.3664"
        },
        {
          "quote": "whether the ownership interest of the underlying assets of the Tokenised Securities are legally valid under the applicable governing laws, whether there are regular reconciliations between the records for the extrinsic rights and the records for the distributed ledger tokens, or whether any encumbrance affects the extrinsic rights.",
          "unit_id": "tokenised-securities.appendix"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "legal_correctness_established": false,
  "question_id": "p10",
  "rival_readings": [
    "Allocation reading: paragraph 10 primarily preserves ultimate accountability despite delegation; separate provisions specify substantive performance requirements. This does not make contractual allocation proof of overall compliance.",
    "Conduct reading: responsibility requires effective management and oversight. The text supports attention to management but does not prescribe a complete diligence or supervision test.",
    "Outcome or combined reading: references to operational soundness and ownership records support assessing substantive results alongside authority and conduct. The retained text does not settle whether paragraph 10 imposes a strict outcome guarantee or how these dimensions combine into a sufficient test."
  ],
  "unresolved": [
    "The legal effect of 'should' and the applicable standard for concluding that the continuing requirement has been fulfilled for a specified period.",
    "Whether authority allocation, supervisory conduct and outcomes are independently necessary, jointly sufficient, or assessed under another legally justified test.",
    "Actual entity, product, outsourcing, governance, supervision and operational facts are absent.",
    "The proposed evidence categories are assessment suggestions, not a source-prescribed exhaustive checklist or safe harbour.",
    "Candidate rules and reported solver acceptance do not establish the truth of interpretive premises or legal fulfillment."
  ]
}
